  "context_pill": "Capital Gains: HUF Partition & Cost Substitution",
  "example_summary": "Mr. A sells a plot of land for ₹ 15,00,000 in June 2025, which he originally received through an HUF partition. We must determine the taxable Long Term Capital Gain by substituting the previous owner's cost and accounting for improvement expenses.",
  "source_provision": "### Statutory Summary Matrix: Capital Gains Acquisition & Improvement\n\n| Statutory Provision | Governing Rule / Condition | Tax Treatment / Legal Consequence |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Section 49(1)** | Acquisition through HUF partition, gift, or succession | Cost to previous owner is adopted as the Cost of Acquisition. |\n| **Section 2(42A)** | Period of holding in specified modes u/s 49(1) | Includes the period for which the asset was held by the previous owner. |\n| **Section 55(2)(b)** | Cost of improvement for capital assets | Capital improvements incurred prior to 1st April 2001 are ignored. |\n\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Partition of HUF: Partition of a Hindu Undivided Family resulting in allotment of a capital asset to a coparcener does not constitute a transfer under Section 47, but adopts previous owner's cost under Section 49(1).\n\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Cost of Improvement Trap: Any expenditure incurred towards improvement or capital addition prior to 1st April 2001 must be completely disregarded for capital gain computations.",
